echo "== extraction 56: the render =="
python3 scripts/db.py amend-extraction --extraction-id 56 --field extraction_status --value reviewed --session "$S" \
 --reason "RENDERED AND READ 2026-09-25 -- the first persisted render of this page. Zero-based index 56 of 5dd866a236fb5978.pdf at 200 dpi, attested in the batch-20 manifest as 5dd866a236fb5978.p0056.png. It shows Table 13, 'Maximum Lengths and Slopes for Rampways', four rows (total horizontal projection / projection of each run / maximum rise of a single run / allowable slope): 2 ft / 2 ft / 3 in / 'If slope = 12.5% (1:8) or less steep' WITH superscript c; 8 / 8 / 9 / 'If slope = 10.0% (1:10) or less steep' WITH superscript c; 60 / 30 / 30 / 'If slope = 8.3% (1:12) or less steep', NO marker; 160 / 40 / 30 / 'If slope = 6.25% (1:16) or less steep', NO marker. Footnotes: a 'A rampway may have more than one ramp run; landings are not counted as part of total allowable horizontal projection.'; b 'All slopes taken from a horizontal plane.'; c 'Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walters, 1971).' CLAIMED VALUE CONFIRMED: the warrant is scoped to the 1:8 and 1:10 bands. CLAIM TEXT IS ONE LETTER OFF THE PAGE: the page prints 'Walters', and this row's claim_text says 'Walter' -- the batch-19 normalisation its own retraction R3 named. It is left as written because a changed claim_text is a second row, not an amendment (D-0168), and recorded here instead. extraction_status stays 'reviewed' (a normalised, verbatim-exempt text is not 'verified'). NO condition_on EDGE IS WRITTEN even though Templer's FHWA study is now admitted (REF-01008): this row's own earlier note makes the edge writable 'if either referent is admitted', but whether REF-01008 IS 'Templer, 1977' is unidentified (it is dated May 1980; candidate 115 stays PENDING), so an edge would harden a hypothesis (R15). WHAT THE GEOMETRY ADDS: the two footnote-c rows' run and rise are exactly REF-01008's test ramps 9 (1:8, 2 ft, 3 in) and 6 (8 ft, 9 in = 1:10.67) -- see extraction 66, which records that the second was unacceptable to manual wheelchair users in ascent. The ERIC stamp at the foot of the page is only partly legible; the locator question is GAP-043's."

echo "== GAP-037 =="
python3 scripts/db.py amend-gap --gap-id GAP-037 --session "$S" --append-note "BATCH 20: FOOTNOTE c RENDERED AND READ, AND THE CHAIN QUESTION ANSWERED AS FAR AS THE PAGE CAN ANSWER IT. (1) THE READING IS CONFIRMED ON A PERSISTED RENDER. Zero-based index 56 of the ED184280 scan, rendered at 200 dpi and attested (batch-20 manifest, 5dd866a236fb5978.p0056.png): superscript c on the 1:8 row (2 ft, 3 in rise) and the 1:10 row (8 ft, 9 in rise) only; none on 1:12 (30 ft run, 30 in rise) or 1:16 (40 ft, 30 in). The footnote reads 'Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walters, 1971).' (2) A CORRECTION TO THIS GAP'S OWN 2026-09-20 TEXT: it says the scope reading 'was confirmed independently on a rendered page image' and that 'the copy this project already holds decided it'. No render of index 56 was persisted anywhere before today -- batch 19's renders of this PDF are indices 55 (Table 12), 162 and 167-169 (ls retrieval-log/*/5dd866a236fb5978.p*). The reading was right; the claim that a render stood behind it was not, until now. (3) WHAT IT SETTLES: REF-01005 does not cite Templer or Walter for its 1:12 or 1:16 band. Footnote c is not a warrant for 1:12. (4) WHAT IT ADDS, AND IT CUTS BOTH WAYS: the two footnote-c rows reproduce EXACTLY the run and rise of two test ramps in Templer's FHWA short-ramp study (REF-01008, admitted this batch) -- ramp 9 (1:8, 2 ft, 3 in curb) and ramp 6 (8 ft, 9 in curb, i.e. 1:10.67). That is consistent with footnote c's Templer being the Georgia Tech ramp work, tested August-November 1976. But ramp 6 was UNACCEPTABLE to manual wheelchair users in ascent in Templer's own data (58 percent against his ~80 percent line, extraction 66), and his Table 20 recommends 1:10 only up to a 6 in curb (extraction 61): Table 13's 1:10 band is more permissive than the study its footnote appears to rest on. (5) WHAT IT DOES NOT SETTLE: which document 'Templer, 1977' is. There are now three candidates and none is chosen -- the 1974 Columbia stairs dissertation REF-01005's bibliography lists; the FHWA ramp study (REF-01008, reported May 1980); and a forthcoming Templer book, 'Stairs and Ramps' (Dowden, Hutchinson and Ross), which NBS IR 78-1554 cites six times (read this batch, candidate 117 rehomed). Candidate 115 stays PENDING. (6) TEMPLER BEARS ON 1:12 INDEPENDENTLY, AND ONLY FOR SHORT RISES: Table 20 recommends 1:12 up to a 9 in curb (extraction 62), and 1:12 over a 6 in rise and 6 ft run was acceptable to every manual wheelchair user in ascent (extraction 65). That does not contradict REF-01005's 1:12 failures over a longer run, because the parameter is length- and rise-conditioned (extraction 63). Templer is evidence ON 1:12; he is not a node OF REF-01005's 1:12 warrant. (7) WALTER: exhausted under the 2026-09-25 owner ruling and not queued for anyone. REF-01008 gives a second, independent account of Walter's recommendations (extraction 64: 1:16 over 20 ft for self-propelled wheelchairs) -- corroboration of the REPORT of Walter, so clause (5) stands: Walter is counted once, and only as reported." 2>&1 | tail -2

echo "== candidates 109 and 110: exhausted, not queued =="
python3 scripts/db.py resolve-candidate --candidate-id 109 --disposition PENDING-VERIFICATION --session "$S" \
 --redescription "EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, AND NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE. $RULING Full text of Walter 1971 is not reachable from anywhere this project can go. ROUTES RECORDED AS TRIED, in this row and in batches 17-19's retrieval logs: Open Library (the bibliographic record OL4653562M, no digitised copy; by title 0, by publisher 99 with the record among them); Internet Archive (no copy); HathiTrust (Cloudflare interstitial); Jisc Library Hub Discover (HTTP 403, Cloudflare, twice); Google Books (quota refused, never asked; quota value 0). The 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports re-checking every legitimate self-service route and finding none that works; its requests were not persisted in this repository's retrieval log, so they are not itemised here. THE EARLIER NEXT STEP IS WITHDRAWN: batch 19 closed this row by naming a request-based route with the catalogue identifiers it had found; under the ruling that route is not a task for any person, human or agent, and nothing in this row should be read as one. THE BIBLIOGRAPHIC RECORD STANDS (Disabled Living Foundation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter). WHAT THE PROJECT HOLDS OF WALTER INSTEAD: two independent second-hand accounts -- REF-01005 page 163 (extraction 54) and, new this batch, REF-01008 printed page 4 (extraction 64): an adjustable indoor laboratory ramp, 20 ft at 1:8.6 to 1:16 and 10 ft at 1:5.5 to 1:10, four-point rating scale, recommending for self-propelled wheelchairs 1:9 over 10 ft and 1:16 over 20 ft. Two readers agreeing about Walter is still Walter counted once. Left PENDING-VERIFICATION rather than retired because the column vocabulary has no 'exhausted' value; the disposition should be read with this note." 2>&1 | tail -3

python3 scripts/db.py resolve-candidate --candidate-id 110 --disposition PENDING-VERIFICATION --session "$S" \
 --redescription "EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, AND NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE. $RULING Full text of the 1957 University of Illinois dissertation is not reachable from anywhere this project can go. ROUTES RECORDED AS TRIED in this row and in batches 17-19's retrieval logs: Open Library 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0; Google Books refused on quota and never asked. The 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports re-checking every legitimate self-service route and finding none that works; its requests were not persisted in this repository's retrieval log, so they are not itemised here. THE EARLIER NEXT STEP IS WITHDRAWN: batch 19 named two repository routes as 'the routes'; under the ruling this row is not a task for any person, human or agent. WHAT STANDS: the 1:8 finding and its 1979 discount on sampling (REF-01005 extraction 57), and the AUTHOR CONTEST, now read on rendered pages of both sources: REF-01005's bibliography prints 'Elmer, C.D.'; REF-01008's reference list (printed page 299, transcribed from the render and persisted) prints 'Dixon, Charles E. A study to determine the Specifications of Wheelchair Ramps, Unpublished Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Illinois, Champagne, Urbana, 1957.' REF-01008 also gives the method as it read it -- a campus questionnaire, then an adjustable laboratory ramp from 1:8.6 to 1:5, 61 subjects, all university students, a 30 ft run or two 15 ft runs with a landing, a five-point scale; recommended 'A maximum gradient of 1:6 ... and a minimum of 1:8.6', while at 1:6 47 percent rated it hard or very hard. Neither author spelling is preferred. The contest stays open because the document that would settle it is the one given up on." 2>&1 | tail -3

echo "== REF-01002 verification_note =="
python3 scripts/db.py amend-source --ref-id REF-01002 --field verification_note --session "$S" \
 --replacement "EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, NOT QUEUED (2026-09-25). $RULING The article's full text (J Architectural and Planning Research 26(2), Summer 2009, pp. 145-158; no DOI) is not reachable from anywhere this project can go: seven routes failed in batch 17 (GAP-026), batches 18-19 added no working route, and the 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports every legitimate self-service route re-checked with none working. Earlier records named request-based routes as the next step; the ruling withdraws them and none is assigned to any person. verification_status stays UNVERIFIED and verification_disposition stays OPEN, because no sanctioned writer closes a disposition with this reason and none is invented. CONSEQUENCE, UNCHANGED: extraction 45 stays skim/preliminary, determination gate 1 stays unresolved, C10 keeps refusing a determination on this source, and parameter 3 x MOB stays undetermined -- an accepted limitation, not a queued task. AUTHOR NOTE: the publisher's own table of contents for the issue (lockescience.com/TOC262.pdf, persisted in the batch-20 retrieval log as 07da0016d338f0f8.pdf, a born-digital text PDF, rendered and read) names the fourth author 'Jerome M. Welner'. The stored author row says 'Weiner', from TRID. It is NOT corrected: correct-source refuses DOI-less rows, it is the only verb that rewrites an admitted source's author rows, and hand SQL is forbidden. GAP-044 records the writer gap." \
 --reason "verification_note was NULL on a source whose standing is the single most consequential open fact on parameter 3. It now states the 2026-09-25 disposition in the row itself, so a reader of REF-01002 does not have to find GAP-016 and GAP-026 to learn that the retrieval question is closed and why." 2>&1 | tail -3

